# From Symmetry to Shell Closure

A periodic pattern begins with a counting question: how many independent states share an energy? Move through the interactive narrative from angular degeneracy to exact closure laws, and then test where ideal symmetry ceases to match nature. For background, see the [quantum-number overview](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_number) and the [spectral theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_theorem).

## 1. Build a Coulomb shell

The $n$th ideal Coulomb shell contains $2n^2$ states after spin doubling. Explore the quadratic capacity and cubic cumulative count.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting proof</summary>The magnetic labels in subshell $l$ run from $-l$ through $l$, giving $2l+1$ states. Summing odd numbers gives $\sum_{l=0}^{n-1}(2l+1)=n^2$, and spin doubles this to $2n^2$. Summing over shells gives $C_n=n(n+1)(2n+1)/3$.</details>

{{visualization:0}}

## 2. Compare the nuclear oscillator

The isotropic oscillator instead assigns capacity $(N+1)(N+2)$ to level $N$. Its first three closures match nuclear magic numbers, then the simple model diverges.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal why the oscillator degeneracy has this form</summary>The number of nonnegative triples with $n_x+n_y+n_z=N$ is $ inom{N+2}{2}$. Two spin choices turn this into $(N+1)(N+2)$. Summation gives $M_n=(n+1)(n+2)(n+3)/3$.</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Make the spectrum literal

A diagonal Hamiltonian with entries $E_j=-1/(j+1)^2$ has standard basis eigenvectors and trace equal to the sum of its energies.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the linear-algebra argument</summary>A real diagonal matrix is Hermitian. Multiplying it by $e_j$ selects the $j$th diagonal entry, so $H e_j=E_j e_j$. Repeating an energy according to shell capacity realizes degeneracy directly.</details>

{{visualization:2}}

## 4. Let disagreement guide the next model

Use the mismatch finder to locate the first point at which idealized closures differ from observed noble-gas or nuclear sequences. Learn more about the [Madelung rule](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aufbau_principle#Madelung_energy_ordering_rule) and the [nuclear shell model](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_shell_model).

{{algorithm:2}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Expert perspective: what remains invariant?</summary>Exact eigenvalues shift under perturbation, but cumulative ranks of isolated spectral clusters remain stable while positive gaps persist. This suggests replacing exact degeneracy by spectral-projection rank in robust models.</details>

